Subject: Re: How to estimate individual encounter probabilities when sigma estimates for both mixture classes differ?
Message-ID: <hHdXekFlPeo.6u5-haQ0CwAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <hHdXekFlPeo.4rOaEiQhCwAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
References: <hHdXekFlPeo.4rOaEiQhCwAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/secrgroup/c/hHdXekFlPeo/m/6u5-haQ0CwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The heterogeneity model is a 2-class finite mixture model, meaning that variation in the detection parameter is modelled as if it resulted from 2 distinct classes of individual. The estimates relate to the 2 classes; note also the mixing parameter pmix. I suppose you want the weighted average of the two (weighted by pmix). See also https://www.otago.ac.nz/density/pdfs/secr-finitemixtures.pdf

