Thanks! And the respective SE is calculated the same way?
Concerning the 2-class models, is it possible to use the model.average on models with different detection functions? And is it possible to average over a model with a single session and another model with 2 sessions? I couldn't find an answer in recent publications so far.
Maybe you have an advice for me on where to read more about this topic.

Am Dienstag, 14. Januar 2020 17:58:54 UTC-1 schrieb Murray Efford:
> It's a mistake to divide by 2. Otherwise, yes.
>
> On Tuesday, January 14, 2020 at 11:37:36 PM UTC+13, IntoTheBlue574 wrote:
> > So in this example would the weighted mean be calculated like this?
> >
> > (1.227834e+04 * 5.357822e-02 + 2.563491e+03 * 9.464218e-01) / 2 = 1.541998e+03
> >
> > Am Montag, 13. Januar 2020 23:33:55 UTC-1 schrieb Murray Efford:
> > > The heterogeneity model is a 2-class finite mixture model, meaning that variation in the detection parameter is modelled as if it resulted from 2 distinct classes of individual. The estimates relate to the 2 classes; note also the mixing parameter pmix. I suppose you want the weighted average of the two (weighted by pmix). See also https://www.otago.ac.nz/density/pdfs/secr-finitemixtures.pdf

