From ub85d22558143e510 at groups.google.com  Thu Jan 16 00:17:50 2020
From: ub85d22558143e510 at groups.google.com (Andrea)
Date: Thu, 16 Jan 2020 00:17:50 +0000
Subject: bk, buffer size, sigma and density
Message-ID: <gKHP5SJ4f6w.aIRxEz7zCwAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/secrgroup/c/gKHP5SJ4f6w/m/aIRxEz7zCwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all -

I'm looking for some feedback on the effect bk is having in my model. I am using secr 4.1.0 and am working with camera trap data from wolverines. 77 detections of 7 animals.

I have run a series of fairly basic models, and the model D~1, g0~bk, sigma~1 is identified as the most parsimonious model by AICc.

                      model                                   detectfn     npar    logLik           AIC          AICc         dAICc   AICcwt
m2                D~1 g0~bk sigma~1             halfnormal    4      -387.0837    782.167    802.167    0.000        1
m7                D~1 g0~bk + T sigma~1       halfnormal    5       -385.2195   780.439    840.439   38.272       0
m5                D~1 g0~bk + Type sigma~1 halfnormal    5      -386.0878    782.176    842.176   40.009       0
m1                D~1 g0~b sigma~1               halfnormal    4      -440.6641    889.328    909.328  107.161      0
m0                D~1 g0~1 sigma~1               halfnormal    3      -454.8092    915.618    923.618  121.451      0
m4                D~1 g0~T sigma~1               halfnormal    4      -452.8202    913.640    933.640  131.473      0
m3                D~1 g0~Type sigma~1         halfnormal    4      -453.3662    914.732    934.732  132.565      0

In all models I used a mask that included a 40 km buffer around trap locations. The suggest.buffer function recommended a 31 km buffer. RPSV(CHBM, CC = TRUE) = 6814.49, so 4 times that recommends ~28 km buffer. Other literature had used 40 km, so I rounded up and went with a 40 km buffer to be safe. Spacing was 2500.

I then used the code:
mask.check(m2, buffers = c(30000, 40000, 50000), spacings = c(1500, 2500, 3500), LL = FALSE, file = "m2.maskcheck")
to evaluate the influence of buffer size on density, and ideally confirm that I had used an appropriate buffer size.

With those 3 buffer sizes, buffer has an impact:

I then used esa.plot to investigate things further and compared esa.plot of m2 to the null model.

Clearly, there's a pretty big difference between these plots. I looked into other models and a buffer of 40 km is sufficient for all other models without the bk covariate. For models with bk it seems that a buffer size of about 80 km is needed before density estimates stabilize. The bk covariate also has a big effect on density and sigma estimates. I would have expected bk to increase density estimates, but instead they are lower. Sigma is much higher - which would explain the need for a larger buffer.

Null model parameters, D~1, g0~1, sigma~1
             link     estimate           SE.estimate          lcl                         ucl
D          log     1.398627e-05    5.551335e-06      6.608876e-06      2.959895e-05
g0         logit   2.330294e-02    3.947773e-03     1.669913e-02       3.243214e-02
sigma   log     7.589314e+03   5.797354e+02     6.535445e+03     8.813124e+03

m2 model parameters, D~1, g0~bk, sigma~1
$`bk = 0`
           link      estimate             SE.estimate           lcl                        ucl
D          log     5.758101e-06     2.344267e-06       2.672592e-06     1.240583e-05
g0         logit   4.181946e-03     1.749903e-03      1.839603e-03      9.478440e-03
sigma   log     2.448864e+04     3.472138e+03    1.857276e+04     3.228886e+04

$`bk = 1`
            link     estimate             SE.estimate          lcl                        ucl
D         log      5.758101e-06     2.344267e-06      2.672592e-06     1.240583e-05
g0        logit    1.943967e-01     6.816278e-02      9.323484e-02     3.615555e-01
sigma   log     2.448864e+04    3.472138e+03     1.857276e+04    3.228886e+04

I reran the model using a mask with a larger buffer (90 km). Under this model, confidence intervals of parameter estimates increase.
$`bk = 0`
            link     estimate              SE.estimate          lcl                         ucl
D         log      7.070365e-06     6.128653e-06       1.630192e-06      3.066515e-05
g0        logit    3.548623e-03     1.512013e-03       1.538013e-03      8.166162e-03
sigma   log     1.923345e+04     7.228713e+03     9.432973e+03     3.921623e+04

$`bk = 1`
             link     estimate             SE.estimate          lcl                        ucl
D          log      7.070365e-06    6.128653e-06      1.630192e-06     3.066515e-05
g0        logit    1.504353e-01     8.382900e-02      4.667432e-02     3.904027e-01
sigma   log     1.923345e+04    7.228713e+03     9.432973e+03    3.921623e+04

I've never encountered this before and have struggling to think about why the addition of bk on g0 would so substantially increase sigma and the required buffer. I do not have any outliers in the detection data. The impact on density is also the opposite of what I would have expected. I'd be curious if anyone has thoughts on what might be going on here.

Thanks
Andrea

From u2c49652a5796ed9c at groups.google.com  Thu Jan 16 01:13:11 2020
From: u2c49652a5796ed9c at groups.google.com (Ben Augustine)
