Message-ID: <gKHP5SJ4f6w.wez2hkP2CwAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <gKHP5SJ4f6w.aIRxEz7zCwAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
References: <gKHP5SJ4f6w.aIRxEz7zCwAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/secrgroup/c/gKHP5SJ4f6w/m/wez2hkP2CwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I'm sure Murray will have good input about this, but to me, it looks like sigma is on the edge of identifiability under the null model due to the small trapping array extent relative to individual movement. Then, when you include the bk effect, the drop in the first capture p0 estimate leads sigma to not be identifiable without the constraint imposed by the state space buffer. So the sigma estimate depends arbitrarily on the buffer size.

Ben

